//? Title: Addition Chains: How Few Multiplications Make a Power?
//? Subtitle: Optimal chains, lower bounds and sliding windows with datamunge's algorithms::addition_chain
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Computing \(x^e\) by repeated multiplication is a question about *addition
chains*: sequences \(1=c_0<c_1<\dots<c_L=e\) in which each term is the sum of
two earlier ones. We derive the cost of the binary method,
\(\lfloor\log_2 e\rfloor+\nu(e)-1\) multiplications, and prove the lower bound
\(\lceil\log_2 e\rceil\). We also show the factor rule
\(\ell(mn)\le\ell(m)+\ell(n)\), which explains why \(x^{15}\) needs only five
multiplications. Exact shortest chains for every \(e\le 1024\) reproduce the
known record values (OEIS A003064) and the Scholz-Brauer inequality.
Binary is optimal for only 28% of exponents. For large exponents we derive
and measure the cost of the sliding-window method. Window width 4 saves 15%
over binary on 64-bit exponents, which is what cryptographic libraries do.
This report has no dataset: its data are the integers themselves.
)

\toc

> Chains and multiplications

If \(c_k=c_i+c_j\) with \(i,j<k\), then \(x^{c_k}=x^{c_i}\,x^{c_j}\) is one
multiplication of powers already computed. A chain of length \(L\) ending at
\(e\) therefore computes \(x^e\) in \(L\) multiplications, and the smallest
such \(L\) is written \(\ell(e)\). The library offers three chains.

- `algorithms::binary_chain(e)` is left-to-right square-and-multiply.
- `algorithms::window_chain(e, k)` is the sliding-window method.
- `algorithms::addition_chain(e)` is a shortest chain, found by iterative-deepening search for \(e\le 4096\).

`mod_pow_chain()` evaluates \(x^e \bmod m\) along any chain, and
`chain_steps()` lists which two earlier terms form each new one.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::print("| e | binary chain | shortest chain | binary | shortest | lower bound |\n");
  std::print("| ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::uint64_t e : {15, 23, 31, 63, 127, 255}) {
    const auto bin = alg::binary_chain(e), best = alg::addition_chain(e);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", e, bin, best, bin.size() - 1, best.size() - 1,
               alg::chain_lower_bound(e));
  }
  const auto best = alg::addition_chain(15);
  std::print("\nSteps of the shortest chain for 15: ");
  for (const auto& [i, j] : alg::chain_steps(best)) std::print("x^{} = x^{} x^{};  ", best[i] + best[j], best[i], best[j]);
  std::print("\n\n3^15 mod 1000003: chain {}, mod_pow {}\n", alg::mod_pow_chain(3, best, 1'000'003),
             alg::mod_pow(3, 15, 1'000'003));
}
```
