\caption(Binary and shortest chains for a few exponents, with their lengths and the proven lower bound)

> Three facts with short proofs

Write \(\lambda(e)=\lfloor\log_2 e\rfloor\) and \(\nu(e)\) for the number of
1 bits of \(e\).

*The binary method costs \(\lambda(e)+\nu(e)-1\).* Reading the bits of
\(e\) from the top, the method starts at 1 for the leading bit. Each later
bit costs a doubling \(c\mapsto 2c\), and each later 1 bit also costs a
\(+1\). That makes \(\lambda\) doublings and \(\nu-1\) additions.

*Lower bound: \(\ell(e)\ge\lceil\log_2 e\rceil\).* Every term satisfies
\(c_k\le 2c_{k-1}\), because both summands are at most \(c_{k-1}\). By
induction \(c_k\le 2^k\), so \(e=c_L\le 2^L\). If \(e\) is not a power of two,
then \(L\ge\lambda(e)+1\). The bound \(\lambda+1\) is reached only when every
step but one is a doubling. Gioia, Subbarao and Sugunamma (1962) showed that
three or more 1 bits need one step more again. `chain_lower_bound()`
returns

$$ \ell(e)\ \ge\ \lambda(e) + [\nu(e)\ge 2] + [\nu(e)\ge 3]. $$

*Factor rule: \(\ell(mn)\le\ell(m)+\ell(n)\).* Take a chain
\(a_0,\dots,a_r\) for \(m\) and a chain \(b_0,\dots,b_s\) for \(n\). Then
\(a_0,\dots,a_r=m,\ mb_1,\dots,mb_s=mn\) is a chain for \(mn\), because
\(mb_k=mb_i+mb_j\). Its length is \(r+s\). For \(15=3\cdot 5\) this gives
\(\ell(15)\le 2+3=5\), with the chain \(1,2,3,6,12,15\). Binary needs 6,
since \(\lambda=3\) and \(\nu=4\). So the binary method is not optimal.

> Shortest chains for every exponent up to 1024

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/addition_chain_lengths.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> e, best, binary, bound;
  for (std::uint64_t n = 1; n <= 1024; ++n) {
    e.push_back(n);
    best.push_back(alg::addition_chain(n).size() - 1.0);
    binary.push_back(alg::binary_chain(n).size() - 1.0);
    bound.push_back(alg::chain_lower_bound(n));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(e, binary, "binary", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38, 0.5}, 2.0)
      .points(e, best, "shortest", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .lines(e, bound, "lower bound", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
      .y_limits(0, 22)
      .title("Multiplications needed for x^e")
      .x_label("exponent e")
      .y_label("chain length")
      .size(860, 520)
      .save_svg("build/addition_chain_lengths.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/addition_chain_lengths.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Binary chain length, shortest chain length \(\ell(e)\) and the proven lower bound for \(e\le 1024\))
\alttext(
Scatter plot with three bands rising like the logarithm of e. Red binary
points lie on top and spread widely; blue shortest-chain points lie below
them in a narrow band; a black step line, the lower bound, runs at or just
under the blue band.
)

The binary length scatters with the number of 1 bits. The optimum hugs
\(\log_2 e\). The next table checks the search against two published
results. The first is the smallest \(e\) with \(\ell(e)=r\), OEIS A003064.
The second is the Scholz-Brauer inequality
\(\ell(2^n-1)\le n-1+\ell(n)\), which has been verified for all small \(n\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const std::vector<std::uint64_t> oeis{1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 19, 29, 47, 71, 127, 191, 379, 607};
  std::vector<std::uint64_t> first(oeis.size(), 0);
  std::map<int, int> savings;
  int optimal_binary = 0;
  for (std::uint64_t e = 1; e <= 1024; ++e) {
    const int len = alg::addition_chain(e).size() - 1, bin = alg::binary_chain(e).size() - 1;
    if (len < int(first.size()) && first[len] == 0) first[len] = e;
    ++savings[bin - len];
    optimal_binary += bin == len;
  }
  std::print("| r | smallest e with l(e) = r | OEIS A003064 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < oeis.size(); ++r) std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", r, first[r], oeis[r]);

  std::print("\n| n | l(2^n - 1) | n - 1 + l(n) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int n = 2; n <= 10; ++n)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", n, alg::addition_chain((1ULL << n) - 1).size() - 1,
               n - 1 + alg::addition_chain(n).size() - 1);
  std::print("\nBinary is optimal for {} of the 1024 exponents. Multiplications saved by the shortest chain: {}\n",
             optimal_binary, savings);
}
```
